<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pechina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bajjana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pechina, called Bajjana in Arabic, is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andarax">Andarax</a> valley. From the late 9th century it was the base of a self-governing community of Andalusi seafarers trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and it became one of the main ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. It declined after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> raised nearby Almería to the status of a city in 955.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pechina.jpg" alt="Pechina" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduardo Milla · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_cueva_Pechina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
